{
  "abstract": "We prove that a biplanar graph on at most twenty-three vertices admits a nine-coloring if one vertex is universal and a second vertex misses at most two neighbors. The main case has two adjacent exceptional vertices. Failure of a nine-coloring would force two eight-vertex-critical graphs, one at each exception. Comparing their exclusive vertices with the two planar edge bounds shows that their underlying bodies coincide. Deleting the exception with an independent set of its nonneighbors then forces small seven-chromatic subgraphs. At order twenty-three, the remaining subgraph is a four-clique joined to a pentagon. Color reservations, edge cuts and restrictions on how a vertex meets the pentagon join settle disjoint copies, overlapping copies and a unique copy. At order twenty-four, each exception in a counterexample must extend the whole twenty-vertex body to an eight-vertex-critical graph with 79 to 81 edges, and its body nonneighbors must have independence number two. If both exceptions have seven body neighbors, nine colors suffice whenever the neighborhoods have between two and six common vertices. The supplement contains the full order induction, the hand proofs for the overlapping neighborhoods and five reproducible exact arithmetic tables.",
